          finite state spaces,
    \item $\mathfrak{U}_{\text{gold}}$ consists of universes with golden
          bases,
    \item $\mathfrak{U}_{\text{stable}}$ consists of universes whose
          recursion operator $E$ preserves feasibility,
    \item $\mathfrak{U}$ is the full class of Gate-generated universes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Position of Our Universe}
Our universe occupies a distinguished position:


\[
\mathcal{U}_{\text{ours}} \in 
\mathfrak{U}_{\text{gold}} \cap \mathfrak{U}_{\text{stable}},
\]


and is minimal in the sense that no proper subprogram of $G$ generates a
universe with equivalent structural richness.

\medskip
This classification provides the framework for comparing our universe to
all other generable universes and for understanding which features are
necessary, contingent, or emergent.
\section{Stability, Sensitivity, and Safety of Generative Mathematical Systems}
\label{sec:safety}

In this section we analyze the stability and sensitivity properties of a
closed generative mathematical system, with particular attention to the
conditions under which such a system may produce results with significant
practical or structural implications.

\subsection{Stability Under Perturbation}
Let $G$ be the generative program and let $\delta G$ denote a perturbation.
We define stability as:


\[
G \text{ stable} \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\forall \delta G \text{ small},\;
\mathcal{U}(G + \delta G) \sim \mathcal{U}(G).
\]


A system is stable if perturbations do not change the equivalence class
of the resulting universe.

\subsection{Sensitivity of Derived Structures}
Even when $G$ is stable, derived structures may be sensitive. Let
$\pi$ be a projection (e.g.\ spacetime, fields, information). Sensitivity
